"The prognosis is influenced by several adverse factors. Preoperative findings included a high PSA of 190 and a biopsy demonstrating a Gleason 4+3 pattern with perineural invasion. The surgical pathology revealed pT3b disease with extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, and lymph node metastasis. These features indicate a high-risk disease profile.\n\nCountervailing findings are less defined by the provided data, as nodal status was documented as positive metastasis. The difference between the biopsy (Gleason 4+3) and the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 3+4) is noted, which adds complexity. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. Postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent changes are critical factors that would revise this prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a potential event within a range of months. The fixed estimated horizon is 27.12 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 20.34–33.90 months; its coverage has not been validated."
